WEBVTT

NOTE Timing is estimated from script and acoustic pauses; listening review pending.

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:03.990
Valentina’s first instinct
is to shorten her message.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:08.115
Shorter can help, but short
is not the same as useful.

00:00:08.115 --> 00:00:12.095
A vague sentence can be
brief and still fail.

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:17.463
Before drafting an email, chat
message, slide, or meeting update,

00:00:17.463 --> 00:00:21.410
answer three questions. What
does the audience need to know?

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:24.780
What do they need to decide or do?

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.022
Why does it matter now? That
final question creates urgency

00:00:30.022 --> 00:00:32.845
without drama. “We have a
supplier issue” is vague.

00:00:32.845 --> 00:00:37.519
“We need a campaign decision by 3:00
p.m., before the scheduled ads enter

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:41.080
final production” tells the
audience why the timing matters.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.651
The analytical work supports the

00:00:45.651 --> 00:00:49.665
message. It should not
control the message’s order.

00:00:49.665 --> 00:00:53.620
Start with the destination, not
the route you took to get there.
